==============================================================================
 The AquariusOS DaVinci Resolve runtime
==============================================================================

WHAT THIS IS

You are inside a Rocky Linux container. It is a complete, separate Linux
userland sitting on top of AquariusOS, and its only job is to be the kind of
Linux that DaVinci Resolve expects.

There is no DaVinci Resolve in this image as it ships. Blackmagic's licence
does not allow anyone else to hand out their installer, so you download it from
them and AquariusOS installs it into a copy of this image on your own machine.


WHY A CONTAINER AT ALL

Resolve ships its own copy of a library called GLib, version 2.68, inside
/opt/resolve/libs. On a modern Linux, the system's text-drawing library (pango)
is built against GLib 2.80 or newer. Resolve loads the system pango but its own
old GLib; pango asks for something that did not exist in 2021; Resolve dies
before its window appears, with a message about an "undefined symbol".

That is the most common "Resolve does not work on Linux" story, and it comes
back every time a distribution moves GLib forward. Every distribution has a
workaround. None of them are permanent.

Enterprise Linux 9 — Rocky, RHEL, AlmaLinux — is still on GLib 2.68, the same
series Resolve bundles. In here the mismatch is not worked around. It cannot
happen.

Blackmagic's only listed Linux for Resolve is Rocky Linux 8.6. This image is
Rocky 9: one release newer, so that a modern graphics driver works, and still
Enterprise Linux, so that Resolve is at home. It is also the VFX Reference
Platform's CY2027 floor (glibc 2.34), which is what the film industry as a
whole is moving to.


WHY NOT ROCKY 10

Rocky 10 carries a modern GLib, which is exactly the thing this image exists to
avoid. There is also a December 2025 report of DaVinci Resolve opening to a
black window on AlmaLinux 10 — the same Enterprise Linux 10 userland — which
nobody has yet reproduced or explained.

You can build this image on Rocky 10 by passing ROCKY=10, and the build will
tell you plainly that the GLib version no longer matches. It is an experiment,
not the default, and it stays that way until somebody tests it on real hardware.


WHAT IS IN HERE

  Everything Resolve links against on Enterprise Linux — the X11 client stack,
  the Apache Portable Runtime, the OpenCL loader, GLib/GTK/pango, fonts.
  The list, with a comment on each group, is in the repository at
  resolve-runtime/packages-required.txt.

  The Vulkan loader and drivers, so the same container can host the Unreal
  Engine 5 editor, whose own floor is Rocky 8.

  Two scripts of ours:
      aquarius-resolve-setup   installs Resolve from your download
      aquarius-resolve-run     starts it with the right settings

WHAT IS NOT IN HERE

  DaVinci Resolve itself (licensing — see above).

  A graphics driver. The driver comes in from AquariusOS at run time. On an
  NVIDIA machine that is the NVIDIA container toolkit's job and it is already
  set up. On AMD, Resolve needs ROCm's OpenCL, which is a ~24 GB install and is
  NOT configured here — an AMD machine will start Resolve and be told its GPU
  processing mode is unsupported. That is honest, known, and on the roadmap.

  Audio hardware access, beyond what the host passes through.


THE COMMANDS, ON THE HOST

  aq resolve install <file>   set this up from Blackmagic's download
  aq resolve status           what is installed, and can it see the GPU
  aq resolve update <file>    install a newer Resolve over the top
  aq resolve shell            a terminal inside this container
  aq resolve remove           delete the container (your projects are not in it)

  The full guide, written for someone who has never used Linux:
      https://github.com/stoneharborent/aquarius-os
      docs/restart/resolve.md

==============================================================================
